from .common import pick, truth, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['This deck is about one application-layer service, **DNS**, the Domain Name System. It covers why we need names, how the **name space** is organised as a tree of **labels**, what **domains**, **FQDNs** and **PQDNs** are, how the database is spread across a hierarchy of **name servers** (root, primary and secondary servers, caching), and the two ways a name is resolved: **recursive** and **iterative**. The recursive vs. iterative comparison is the most likely written question.'],
    2: ['**DNS** is the Internet’s directory service: a **client-server application** that maps **host names** (like www.psu.edu.sa) to their **IP addresses**. This mapping is called **name resolution** (also name translation, name mapping or address resolution; don’t confuse it with ARP, which maps IP addresses to MAC addresses).',
        'Why use names at all? Because **IP addresses are hard to remember**: people remember google.com, not 142.250.185.46. Names also stay the same when a server moves to a new address.',
        'The problem is that the network itself only understands **numeric addresses**. The solution: let people use alphanumeric names, and add a **distributed, hierarchical** protocol, DNS, that maps between names and IP addresses whenever a program needs to contact a host.'],
    3: ['IP addresses are unique, so host names must be unique too. With billions of hosts, one flat list of names would be impossible to manage. The solution: each name is made of several parts, and each part is called a **label**.',
        'The names form a **tree** with the **root at the top**: this is the **hierarchical name space**. Every node in the tree has a label, and DNS requires that **children of the same node have different labels**. That rule guarantees every full name is unique: two hosts can both be called “www” as long as they sit under different parents.',
        'The hierarchy also lets name assignment be **decentralised**. A central authority, **IANA**, controls the top part of the name, which says what kind of organisation it is (com, net, sa, ca…), and the organisation’s name (ieee, cnn, intel…). Each organisation then manages the **rest** of its names itself (cis, eng, sales…) without asking anyone.'],
    4: ['The **generic** top-level domain labels describe the type of organisation: **com** (commercial), **edu** (education), **gov** (government), **mil** (military), **org** (non-profit), **net** (network support centres), **int** (international organisations), plus **aero**, **biz**, **coop**, **info**, **museum**, **name** (individuals) and **pro** (professionals). The other kind of top-level domain is the **country** domain: sa, us, jp and so on.'],
    5: ['The Internet is divided into **more than 200 top-level domains**. A **domain** is a **subtree** of the name space: a group of hosts under the administrative control of **one single entity**, such as a company or a government agency.',
        'Each domain is divided into **subdomains**, and those can be divided again. The **leaves** of the tree are domains with no subdomains: a leaf can be a single host, or it can stand for a company with thousands of hosts.'],
    6: ['A **domain** is any subtree of the name space, and it can contain smaller domains (subdomains). The **domain name** is the name of the node at the **top** of that subtree: the subtree under “edu” is the edu domain, and inside it, the subtree under “psu” is the psu.edu domain.'],
    7: ['Every node in the tree has a **domain name**: the sequence of **labels separated by dots**, **read from the node up to the root**. For the node aComputer under bDept under topUniversity under edu, the name is **aComputer.bDept.topUniversity.edu.** The final dot stands for the **root**, whose label is empty. So the rightmost label (before the root) is the **top-level domain**.'],
    8: ['An **FQDN** (fully qualified domain name) is the **complete** name, going all the way to the root, and so **ending with a dot**: challenger.atc.fhda.edu. **Only an FQDN can be translated** into an IP address, because only the full name identifies one node unambiguously.',
        'A **PQDN** (partially qualified domain name) is an **incomplete** name, such as “www” or “cs.hmme”, used to refer to a host at the **same site**.',
        'When a user types a PQDN, the **DNS client** (the **resolver**) **completes** it by adding the local suffix (for example “.psu.edu.sa.”) before looking it up. That is why you can often type just a short name inside an organisation.'],
    9: ['Where should all these names and addresses be stored? One **central** server would be **inefficient and unreliable**: it would receive **heavy traffic** from requests all over the world, its **failure** would make every name unavailable, and it would be **hard to maintain**.',
        'So DNS is a **distributed database**: the information is spread over a large number of computers called **name servers**, organised **hierarchically** and located all over the world. **No single host** holds all the mappings for every host on the Internet; each server knows its own part and where to find the others.'],
    10: ['The name servers follow the shape of the name tree. At the top is the **root server**; below it, servers for the **top-level domains** (edu, com, us…); below those, servers for individual domains (fhda.edu, bk.edu, mcgraw.com, irwin.com). Each server is responsible for its part of the tree and knows how to reach the servers below it.'],
    11: ['A domain usually has two kinds of name server. The **primary** server loads all its information from a **disk file** on its own machine; it is where the domain’s records are created and updated. The **secondary** server loads all its information **from the primary** server, keeping a copy so the domain still works if the primary fails or is overloaded. When the secondary downloads the information from the primary, that is called a **zone transfer**.'],
    12: ['A **root server** holds **references to the top-level domain servers**: it doesn’t know every name, but it knows which server is responsible for com, for sa, for edu, and so on. There are **13 root servers** (named A to M), distributed all over the world (each is actually many machines sharing one address).'],
    13: ['Every time a server gets a query for a name outside its own domain, it would have to search for the right server, which takes time. DNS reduces this with **caching**: when a server receives an answer, it **remembers** it for a while. The next time anyone asks for the same name, it can answer straight from its cache. (Cached answers expire after a set time, so out-of-date information doesn’t last forever.)'],
    14: ['How an application uses DNS: a program such as a file transfer client has a host name but needs an IP address. It passes the name to the **DNS client** on the same machine; the DNS client sends a **query** to a DNS server, receives a **response** with the IP address, and returns the address to the program, which can then open the connection.'],
    15: ['DNS is a client-server application. The **client** part is the **resolver**; the **server** part is the **name server** (whose host name typically starts with the label “DNS”, as in DNS.psu.edu.sa).',
         'The resolver receives a request from an application-layer program (such as HTTP) as a system call, and returns the answer in a format the local host can use.',
         'There are two ways to resolve a name. The first is **recursive resolution**: host A asks its **local name server** B. If B has the answer in its cache or database, it replies. Otherwise B asks a **root** server; if the root doesn’t know, it asks a server at the **next level** (C) itself, and so on down the tree until some server has the answer. The answer is then passed **back along the same chain** to host A. In recursive resolution, each server takes over the job of finding the answer for the one that asked it.'],
    16: ['The example: host lab.CS.PSU.EDU.SA wants the IP address of the web server Sales.mcgraw.com. In steps 1 to 4 the query is passed along: the host asks DNS.PSU.EDU.SA, which asks the root, which asks DNS.com, which asks DNS.mcgraw.com. In steps 5 to 8 the answer comes back the same way, server by server, to the host.'],
    17: ['The steps in words: (1) the host asks its **local name server**, DNS.PSU.EDU.SA. (2) It doesn’t know, so it asks the **root** server. (3) The root doesn’t know, so **the root asks** DNS.com. (4) DNS.com doesn’t know, so it asks **DNS.McGraw.com**, which has the answer. (5–8) DNS.McGraw.com answers DNS.com, which answers the root, which answers the local server, which answers the host. The key point: each server asks the next one **on behalf of** the server that asked it.'],
    18: ['The second technique is **iterative resolution**. Host A asks its local name server B. If B doesn’t have the answer, it asks a **root** server.',
         'But this time, a root server that doesn’t know the answer doesn’t ask anyone else. It **replies to B** with the **IP address of the name server at the next level** (C): in effect, “I don’t know, ask C”.',
         'The **local name server B** then sends its query **directly** to C, and the process repeats: each server either answers or gives B the address of the next one, until B gets the IP address that was asked for.',
         'Finally B sends the answer to host A. The difference from recursive: in iterative resolution the **local server does all the asking**, and the other servers only give referrals. Memory aid: recursive = “I’ll find out for you”; iterative = “ask him instead”.'],
    19: ['The same lookup done iteratively: the local server DNS.PSU.EDU.SA queries the root (steps 2–3), which refers it to DNS.com; it then queries DNS.com (4–5), which refers it to DNS.mcgraw.com; it then queries DNS.mcgraw.com (6–7), which gives the answer; and it answers the host (8). Every arrow starts or ends at the **local server**: the root never talks to DNS.com.'],
    20: ['The steps in words: (1) the host asks the local server. (2) The local server asks the **root**. (3) The root replies with the **address of DNS.com**. (4) The local server asks **DNS.com**. (5) DNS.com replies with the **address of DNS.McGraw.com**. (6) The local server asks **DNS.McGraw.com**. (7) DNS.McGraw.com, being responsible for McGraw.com, replies with the **IP address of sales.McGraw.com**. (8) The local server gives it to the host. So the local server sent three queries itself (root, DNS.com, DNS.McGraw.com).'],
    21: ['DNS can use either **UDP or TCP**, on the well-known port **53**. Normal lookups use **UDP**, because a query and its answer are each short enough to fit in one datagram (Chapter 9); TCP is used for large transfers such as zone transfers between primary and secondary servers.'],
}

